### Microsoft / SharePoint ingestion flow (React → server → DB)

```
1. React initiates Microsoft login:
    ├─ GET /v1/auth/microsoft or /v1/auth/microsoft/login
    ├─ server builds OAuth authorize URL with
    │   MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID, MICROSOFT_REDIRECT_URI, MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID
    │   scopes: offline_access User.Read Files.Read.All Sites.Read.All
    └─ browser redirects to Microsoft consent screen

2. Microsoft OAuth callback:
    ├─ GET /v1/auth/microsoft/callback
    ├─ server exchanges code for access_token + refresh_token
    ├─ server calls Graph /me to get profile
    └─ server saves/updates user row with microsoft_refresh_token

3. React requests SharePoint/OneDrive tree:
    ├─ GET /v1/ingest/sharepoint/tree
    ├─ server calls get_user_microsoft_access_token(user)
    ├─ server calls Graph API on /me/drive/root/children or /me/drive/items/{folder_id}/children
    └─ returns SharePoint/OneDrive items to the UI

4. React selects a file or shared link:
    ├─ POST /v1/ingest/sharepoint with file_id
    └─ POST /v1/ingest/sharepoint/url with sharepoint_url

5. Server begins ingestion:
    ├─ initiate_sharepoint_ingestion() validates dataset ownership
    ├─ confirms microsoft_refresh_token exists
    ├─ fetches item metadata from Graph
    │   - file name, size, quickXorHash, download URL
    ├─ if quickXorHash matches existing mapping:
    │   • reuse existing UploadedFile and complete instantly
    └─ otherwise queue a background job

6. Background download job:
    ├─ acquires Redis lock lock:ingest:sharepoint:{provider_file_id}
    ├─ process_sharepoint_ingestion_job() refreshes Graph access token
    ├─ downloads the file from Graph
    │   - direct @microsoft.graph.downloadUrl or /content endpoint
    ├─ streams bytes into uploads/files/Sharepoint
    ├─ computes SHA-256 master_hash while writing
    └─ writes staging file then renames to final path

7. Final DB commit:
    ├─ if master_hash already exists, reuse UploadedFile
    ├─ else create UploadedFile row:
    │   - filename, file_size_bytes, master_hash, physical_path, source_type=Sharepoint
    ├─ insert DatasetFolderFilesMapping row
    ├─ update AsyncIngestionJob to completed
    └─ sync dataset status from mappings
```

### Key Test Engineering: pg_insert SQLite Workaround

The production `finalize_upload` call uses:
```python
from sqlalchemy.dialects.postgresql import insert as pg_insert
stmt = pg_insert(DatasetFolderFilesMapping).values(...).on_conflict_do_nothing(constraint="uq_dataset_folder_file")
db.execute(stmt)
```

In tests, `file_services.pg_insert` is monkeypatched with:
```python
def sqlite_insert(table):
    class _Proxy:
        def __init__(self, stmt): self._stmt = stmt
        def values(self, **kw): self._stmt = self._stmt.values(**kw); return self
        def on_conflict_do_nothing(self, **kw): return self._stmt  # no-op
    return _Proxy(sa_insert(table))
```

This returns a plain `sa_insert` statement with `on_conflict_do_nothing` ignored. On SQLite, duplicate rows will raise `IntegrityError` (unlike PostgreSQL which silently skips). In tests this is safe because dedup scenarios are constructed so no actual duplicates are inserted during the finalize call itself.

